data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR-Cas9 borrows from a bacterial defense system: a guide RNA locates the target DNA, and the Cas9 protein cuts it there [2].\n- Delivering Cas9 with a synthetic guide RNA into a cell lets scientists cut the genome at a chosen spot, removing existing genes or adding new ones [6].\n- Scientists often use CRISPR to \"knock out\" a gene, and can replace a disease-causing mutation by supplying a repair template [3].\n- In agriculture, CRISPR may edit crops for resistance to bacteria, which could reduce pesticide use [1].\n- For genetic disease, it has been proposed for mutation-caused conditions such as Huntington's disease, which stems from an HTT gene mutation [1]. A linked headline reports the FDA considering a CRISPR sickle cell treatment [5]."}

