data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"analyst","content":"- **Core mechanism:** A guide RNA locates a target DNA sequence, and Cas9 cuts it at that spot [2]. Delivering Cas9 with a synthetic guide RNA lets scientists remove or add genes at a chosen site [6].\n\n- **From disruption to correction:** Researchers often knock out genes, but they can also replace a disease-causing mutation by supplying a repair template [3].\n\n- **Broad applications:** In agriculture, CRISPR may help crops resist bacteria, potentially reducing pesticide use [1]. For genetic disease, it has been proposed for mutation-caused conditions like Huntington's, which stems from an HTT gene mutation [1]. The FDA is reportedly considering a CRISPR sickle cell treatment [5]."}

